<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
A JSON Web Token (JWT) has three base64url-encoded segments separated by <code>.</code>: a header, a payload
with the claims, and a signature. The claims can only be trusted after the signature has been checked against
a trusted key. Anyone can create a token with any payload, for example one that claims an administrator role.
</p>
<p>
This query reports code that splits a token on <code>.</code> and base64-decodes the payload segment
(index <code>1</code>) without evidence that the signature has been verified. Claims that are decoded without
being authenticated can be forged.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Do not parse JWT claims by hand. Use a JWT library such as
<code>System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt.JwtSecurityTokenHandler.ValidateToken</code> or
<code>Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens.JsonWebTokenHandler.ValidateTokenAsync</code>, with
<code>TokenValidationParameters</code> that require a valid signature, and read the claims from the validated result.
</p>
<p>
If the token was already validated somewhere else, for example by a remote authentication service, read the
claims from that validated result, not from the raw token string.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, the payload is decoded and the <code>roles</code> claim is trusted without checking the
signature. An attacker can send a forged token with <code>"roles": ["ADMIN"]</code>:
</p>

<sample src="UnverifiedJwtPayloadDecodeBad.cs" />

<p>
The following example validates the token signature before it reads any claims:
