<sample src="UnverifiedJwtPayloadDecodeGood.cs" />
</example>

<section title="Known limitations">
<ul>
<li>
A read of the signature segment (index <code>2</code>) of the same <code>Split</code> result counts as evidence of
verification only when it, or its base64-decoded value, reaches a comparison or an argument of a method whose name
contains <code>verify</code> or <code>equals</code>. The query does not check that the result of that comparison
is used, or that the comparison is a correct cryptographic check. Logging the segment does not count.
</li>
<li>
Validation by a library is recognized when a call to <code>JwtSecurityTokenHandler.ValidateToken</code> on the same
token dominates the decode, and the <code>TokenValidationParameters</code> object initializer passed to it enables
<code>ValidateIssuerSigningKey</code> and sets <code>IssuerSigningKey</code>. For
<code>JsonWebTokenHandler.ValidateTokenAsync</code>, the decode must also be dominated by an
<code>if (!result.IsValid)</code> check whose branch throws.
</li>
<li>
Code that has the token validated elsewhere before it decodes the payload, for example by a remote service, by
middleware, by a custom helper, or with parameters that are configured indirectly, is still reported.
</li>
</ul>
</section>

<references>
<li>CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/345.html">CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity</a>.</li>
<li>CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/347.html">CWE-347: Improper Verification of Cryptographic Signature</a>.</li>
<li>IETF: <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7519">RFC 7519: JSON Web Token (JWT)</a>.</li>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/JSON_Web_Token_for_Java_Cheat_Sheet.html">JSON Web Token Cheat Sheet</a>.</li>
</references>

</qhelp>
